Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Amend Addenda  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Breast  excisional tissue  Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  JAMES D IGLEHART M D   CASE  BS 07  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME              Addended Report              Resident  Elizabeth Amy Morgan  M D   Eric C Huang  M D   Ph D   Pathologist  Susan Lester  M D   Ph D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   RIGHT BREAST  WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND A MASS AND AXILLARY  DISSECTION  TWO SPECIMENS    RESIDUAL INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  poorly differentiated  is present only  within multiple lymphvascular spaces throughout the specimen  18 of 25 slides    see note   No stromal invasion is seen   A fibrotic tumor bed is present  associated with the prior core needle biopsy  site  The tumor bed is transected at the lateral margin   No carcinoma in situ is present  EIC negative    METASTATIC CARCINOMA is present in 2 of 13 lymph nodes  In one node  there is  a 0 5cm fibrotic area  consistent with an area of treated carcinoma   associated with multiple small clusters of tumor cells  all    0 02cm  approximately 40 to 50 cells total   The second lymph node has a  cluster of five tumor cells  No extranodal extension is present   NOTE  The patient presented with a palpable thickened area in the lateral  breast  Ultrasound showed multiple masses  the largest 1 4cm  and an enlarged  1 7cm lymph node  Core needle biopsy showed a poorly differentiated invasive  carcinoma that was ER positive  and negative for PR and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu  An FNA of  the lymph node was positive  There was erythema associated with the carcinoma   but a skin biopsy did not show carcinoma   The patient received neoadjuvant chemotherapy and the palpable mass resolved   Grossly  there was a focal hemorrhagic area in the region of the clip  1 x 1  cm  that corresponds with the core biopsy site and surrounding tumor bed in the  inferior portion of the specimen  However  tumor bed changes are also present  in the lateral area of the specimen and appear to have been transected at this  margin  Residual tumor in lymphatics is present throughout the majority of the  specimen  This suggests that the carcinoma was extensively present in this  area prior to treatment  The carcinoma in the breast and in the lymph nodes  appears viable  The residual estimated cellularity is 5   However  this is an  unusual case of a carcinoma with low cellularity  as the carcinoma is present  solely in lymphatics   The response would correlate with a Miller Payne response score of 4  The  Residual Cancer Burden is REMOVED_DATE  which corresponds to a RCB II   This carcinoma would be classified as AJCC  6th edition  yp TX  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   i     Immunoperoxidase studies for ER  PR  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  neu will be repeated on  lymphovascular invasion and reported in an addendum   CLINICAL DATA   History  Large tumor in right breast with a positive lymph node  Treated with  pre op chemotherapy with a complete clinical response   Operation  Not specified   Operative Findings  None given   Clinical Diagnosis  Not provided   TISSUE SUBMITTED   A 1  Right lumpectomy for cancer  short   superior  long   lateral  to mammo    B 2  Right axillary contents   RADIOLOGY   Clip  wire and mass in specimen  Dr  Birdwell called Dr  Iglehart in OR at  11 20 AM   GROSS DESCRIPTION   The specimen is received fresh in two parts  each  labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s  name and unit number   Part A    1  Right lumpectomy for cancer  short equals superior  long equals  lateral   consists of a breast biopsy specimen  10 5cm medial to lateral  REMOVED_DATE  cm inferior to superior  and 5 0cm anterior to posterior  with a localization  wire in place and a short suture marking the superior margin and a long suture  marking the lateral margin  An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates  an ill defined mass and a clip next to the localization wire  whic