The patient appeared well developed   well nourished  and in no acute distress   A 0 5cm tender  well defined  round  firm and mobile nodule was palpated at the  end of the surgical scar in postero lateral chest wall     Ultrasound was used to evaluate the lesion but the nodule was not visible     Assessment  Based on the clinical history and physical examination  an FNAB is  warranted     Procedure  The procedure and potential complications were explained to the  patient  who understood the purpose  risks  and benefits of the procedure  The  patient confirmed name  date of birth  laterality  and lesion to be aspirated   Written consent was obtained  The biopsy procedure was performed by palpation       1 aspirate was performed using 25 gauge needles with local anesthesia and  without complications   1 slide was stained for immediate evaluation  Additional tissue was submitted  for further evaluation     The procedure was performed by  Rosemary Tambouret  M D    Disposition  The immediate interpretation finding was discussed with the  patient  Michelle Specht  M D  was notified of the preliminary results by e mail  at 7 45 am         The signing pathologist attests that he she performed the procedure or  if  performed by a resident or fellow  directed and supervised the FNA procedure and  was immediately available to further assist the resident fellow before  during  and after the procedure  and has reviewed and confirmed the interpretation of  slides stained for immediate evaluation           Electronically Signed Out By  Rosemary H Tambouret MD on REMOVED_DATE 06 48      CLINICAL HISTORY  Left chest wall nodule at end of scar  Clinically suspect fat necrosis        SPECIMEN SOURCE  A  SUBCUTANEOUS MASS FNA BIOPSY  LEFT CHEST WALL    GROSS DESCRIPTION  A  SUBCUTANEOUS MASS FNA BIOPSY  LEFT CHEST WALL  Received 1 slides in ETOH   Received 1 air dried slides         Specimen  SUBCUTANEOUS MASS FNA BIOPSY  LEFT CHEST WALL       report_end  , not_surgery.
