Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES   Accessioned On  REMOVED_DATE  CARRASCO  RUBEN  D   M D  PH D   DIAGNOSIS  by CARRASCO RUBEN D  M D  PH D   LEFT BREAST  CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY   ATYPICAL LYMPHOID INFILTRATE  See note   The specimen consists of breast tissue containing predominantly stroma  and scant amount of ductal epithelium  The stroma is very focally involved  by an atypical lymphoid infiltrate consisting of large cells present in  small cluster  The large atypical cell are characterized by a vesicular  nuclei  prominent  1 to 3 nucleoli and moderate amount of cytoplasm  Rare  apoptotic bodies and mitotic figures are noted among the large atypical  cells   Small lymphocytes are also present and occur primarily around breast  epithelial structures   Immunoperoxidase studies performed on paraffin tissue sections reveal the  large cells to be reactive for LCA   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and the B cell markers  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID a and weakely positive for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   very faint staining   Substantial  tissue was lost from the slide stained for bcl 6  but nuclear positivity  was seen in the large cells  Staining for immunoglobulin kappa   lambda  light chains is difficult to interprete due to high background but appear  to show an excess of lambda membrane staining of the large cells  The  small lymphocytes are positive for LCA   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and the T cell marker  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    Staining for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  highlights endothelial cells and some fibrous stromal  cells  Staining for Cytokeratin  panK highlights breast epithelium   NOTE  The infiltrate is atypical and composed of large cells reactive  for LCA   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and the B cell markers  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID a  The findings are  highly suspicious for involvement of the breast tissue by large B cell  lymphoma  however  given the limited nature of this specimen  the sparse  atypical infiltrate present  and an equivocal staining for immunoglobuling  light chains  a definitive diagnosis is not possible  Suggest correlation  with additional clinical information and laboratory studies including  additional tissue sampling of another involved site if clinically  indicated   CLINICAL DATA   History  FNA of left breast at MGH showed atypical lymphoid proliferation   favor large cell type   Operation  11 G Ultrasound guided  Operative findings  Vague hypoechoic mass  Clinical Diagnosis  Lyphoma  Breast image findings  ill defined   TISSUE SUBMITTED  1  Left breast tissue in a yellow cassette  GROSS DESCRIPTION  by LAZAR ALEXANDER J M D  PH D   The specimen is received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit  number  and  Lt  breast   in a single yellow cassette  and consists of  multiple  soft  fragmented  yellow to white tissue cores  1 4 x REMOVED_DATE 2cm in  aggregate    Micro 1  Yellow cassette labeled  Lt  breast   Multi frags  ESS   Reports to  GADD  MICHELE  A M D   SPECIMEN TYPE  BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY IES    report_end  , Excisional_biopsy_(Lumpectomy).
