Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General Hospital  Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID  Tel  617  REMOVED_DATE    Molecular Pathology Report  MSI Analysis      Accession     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  E   DOB  REMOVED_DATE  Age  41   Sex  F  MRN   REMOVED_CASE_ID   Institution  MGH  Location  YAW 7  GI CC  Date of Collection  REMOVED_DATE  Date of Accession  REMOVED_DATE  Reported  REMOVED_DATE 12 56  Results To   Jill N Allen MD  Joseph Misdraji MD    CLINICAL HISTORY   Gastrointestinal Tract  Stomach Gastric  Large Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma    SPECIMENS RECEIVED   A  Molecular test for MSI    GROSS DESCRIPTION   2 blocks from Massachusetts General Hospital  the archived specimens were  retrieved and reviewed for adequacy    SLIDE BLOCK DESCRIPTION    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   tumor   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   normal             TEST   MICROSATELLITE INSTABILITY ANALYSIS    INDICATION FOR TEST  Gastrointestinal Tract  Stomach Gastric  Large Cell  Neuroendocrine Carcinoma    SPECIMEN S  TESTED   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Massachusetts General  Hospital  Boston  MA  United States     RESULTS     LOCUS STABILITY     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID c4 Stable    MYCL Stable     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Stable      INTERPRETATION   The data are consistent with a microsatellite stable tumor by microsatellite  sizing analysis     COMMENT   Loss of mismatch repair protein expression and shifts in the size of germline  microsatellite repeats in somatic tumor specimens  microsatellite instability or  MSI  are the hallmarks of tumors with defects in DNA mismatch repair proteins   Such tumors  especially colorectal and endometrial carcinoma  and including  ovarian  arise in patients with the Hereditary Non Polyposis Colon Cancer   HNPCC  syndrome  Ninety to ninety five percent of colon tumors from HNPCC  patients show microsatellite instability     TEST INFORMATION   Immunohistochemistry for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and analysis of  microsatellite repeat sizes in tumors from young patients with colon or  endometrial or ovarian cancer and a family history of such tumors is an  effective screen for germline mutations in mismatch repair genes  If there is  loss of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  or  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  immunostaining and there are shifts in germline  microsatellite repeat sizes in tumor DNA  sequence analysis of the  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  genes may be indicated     Genomic DNA was extracted from tumor and non tumor control tissues  PCR with  fluorescent primers was performed for the following five MSI consensus  microsatellite loci  the Bethesda markers     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID     REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Testing with the other five markers   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   MYCL   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   is also performed if indicated  Repeat sizing and patterns are  analzyed by capillary electrophoresis using the Applied Biosystems  REMOVED_CASE_ID xL  instrument  Instability of repeat sizes patterns in the tested markers is  graded as follows  none   microsatellite stable  MSS   0  but 30  is MSI Low   and  30  markers is MSI High     1  Gryfe  R   H  Kim  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID    Tumor microsatellite instability and  clinical outcome in young patients with colorectal cancer   N Engl J Med 342 2    69 77     2  Lindor  N  M   L  J  Burgart  et al    REMOVED_CASE_ID    Immunohistochemistry versus  microsatellite instability testing in phenotyping colorectal tumors   J Clin  Oncol 20 4   REMOVED_DATE    3  Umar  A   et al   Revised Bethesda Guidelines for hereditary nonpolyposis  colorectal cancer  Lynch syndrome  and microsatellite instability  J Natl Cancer  Inst   REMOVED_CASE_ID   96 4   p  REMOVED_DATE    This test was developed  and its performance characteristics were determined by  the MGH Diagnostic Molecular Pathology Laboratory  It has not been cleared or  approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  The FDA has determined that  such clearance or approval is not necessary  This test is used for clinical  purposes  Pursuant to the requirements of CLIA  88  this laboratory has  established and verified the test s accuracy and precision     Questions concerning these values should be addressed to Dr  A  John Iafrate at   617  REMOVED_DATE MGH internal address  Center for Integrated Diagnostics   Massachusetts General Hospital  55 Fruit St   Jackson  REMOVED_CASE_ID   Boston  MA  REMOVED_CASE_ID           Electronically Signed Out By  Valentina Nardi MD         PART   Molecular test for MSI       report_end  , Excisional_biopsy_(Lumpectomy).
